  {
   "id": "e2-87",
   "name": "Conditionals: zero, first and second"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A conditional sentence has an if-clause (the condition) and a main clause (the result).\n• Zero conditional (always true, facts): If + present, present. “If you heat water to 100 °C, it boils.”\n• First conditional (a real possibility in the future): If + present, will + verb. “If it rains, the festival will be cancelled.”\n• Second conditional (an unreal or unlikely situation): If + past, would + verb. “If I had a guitar, I would play every day.” With be, use “were”: “If I were rich, I would build a school.”\nThe if-clause can come first (with a comma) or second (no comma): “The festival will be cancelled if it rains.”\nNever put “will” or “would” in the if-clause.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Complete with the correct form: “If it ___ (rain) tomorrow, we ___ (stay) at home.”",
  "steps": [
   "Tomorrow is a real future possibility: first conditional.",
   "If + present (rains), will + verb (will stay)."
  ],
  "answer": "If it rains tomorrow, we will stay at home.",
  "checks": []
 },
